ass  | 408.183778013 Da  | PubChem\nTopological Polar Surface Area  | 55.1 Å²  | PubChem\nHeavy Atom Count  | 31  | PubChem\nFormal Charge  | 0  | PubChem\nComplexity  | 559  | PubChem\nIsotope Atom Count  | 0  | PubChem\nDefined Atom Stereocenter Count  | 0  | PubChem\nUndefined Atom Stereocenter Count  | 1  | PubChem\nDefined Bond Stereocenter Count  | 0  | PubChem\nUndefined Bond Stereocenter Count  | 0  | PubChem\nCovalently-Bonded Unit Count  | 1  | PubChem\nCompound Is Canonicalized  | Yes  | PubChem; Computed by PubChem (release 2021.05.07); Context: Whether the compound has successfully passed PubChem's valence bond canonicalization procedure. Some large, complex, or highly symmetric structures may fail this process.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — transparency log for MCP tool definitions (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/log)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; ### heldfast ...     curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\ ... MCP tool defs](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/badge/<id>.svg)](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/s/<id>)\n\nOther logs, the same servers\n\n## Outside checks\n\nHISTOR is not the only public record of MCP tool definitions. Where another log publishes its reads, the two are compared on the same servers and days, and the result is published both ways, with HISTOR's own misses.\n\n### ScraperWatch\n\nA daily diff of a fixed panel of about 3,000 remote endpoints. HISTOR compares each of their published days with its own reads, and ScraperWatch shows the result on its page under \"Outside check\". The first comparison found a robots.txt parsing limit on their side and a handshake regression on HISTOR's; both were fixed the same day.\n\nscraperwatch.com/mcp-changes\n/datasets/peers/scraperwatch/latest\n\n### heldfast\n\nAn open daily record of the tool definitions of the servers in the official MCP registry. Of the 23,190 servers both logs recorded on 26 October 2026, 21,215 had every field in both; on those, 97.9% of servers and 99.1% of tools were identical byte for byte, and each difference has a stated cause: package versions, read times, failed reads, a first page only, an older protocol.\n\ngithub.com/rufat325/heldfast\n\nFor clients, registries and server authors\n\n## Use it\n\nBefore your agent trusts a server, send the tools it received. The answer is signed by the log's did:key and names the tree head it was given under.\n    \n    curl -sS https://histor.modelmarket.dev/api/v1/check \\\n      -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n      -d '{\"endpoint\":\"https://example.com/mcp\",\"tools\":[ …tools/list result… ],\"contribute\":true}'\n    # -> {\"type\":\"histor.check/v1\",\"match\":\"same\"|\"different\"|\"previously-observed\"|…,\n    #     \"observed\":{\"unchangedSince\":\"…\",\"changes\":0}, \"patternScan\":{…}, \"signature\":{…}}\n    \n    # README badge for your server\n    [![MCP tool defs](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/badge/<id>.svg)](https://histor.modelmarket.dev/s/<id>)\n    \n    # Atom feed of every observed change\n    https://histor.modelmarket.dev/feed.xml\n\nWARDEN checks definitions at connect\n\nHISTOR remembers what was advertised\n\nTHEMIS admits at publish\n\nHub lists histor.check@v1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo public records of MCP tool definitions, one day, 23,190 servers: where they agree (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/two-public-records-of-mcp-tool-definitions-one-day-23190-servers-where-they-agree/)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 6 days ago; Several projects now keep a public record of what MCP servers tell models about their tools: HISTOR (ours), heldfast, rugsnare, and signed per-tool grades from AgentAvow. ... Data, scripts and pinned inputs: https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/interop-digests-2026-10-31/.\n\n31 Oct 2026 · 6 min read\n\n# Two public records of MCP tool definitions, one day, 23,190 servers: where they agree\n\n#mcp#ai#security#opensource\n\nImage\n\nSeveral projects now keep a public record of what MCP servers tell models about their tools: HISTOR (ours), heldfast, rugsnare, and signed per-tool grades from AgentAvow. Each hashes a tool definition its own way. If two of them read the same server, do they see the same thing, and can one answer in the other's format?\n\nOn 26 October HISTOR published its weekly signed snapshot at 13:49:50Z. heldfast checkpointed its feed at 13:48:36Z the same day. Both are public, so the comparison below can be rerun from the pinned inputs.\n\n## The formats\n\nAll five use SHA-256 over sorted-key JSON, and four over RFC 8785. They differ in which fields count and in how \"missing\" is written:\n\n | name  | title  | description  | inputSchema  | outputSchema  | annotations  | icons\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nHISTOR tool-set digest  | yes  | beside it  | yes  | yes  | yes  | beside it  | beside it\nheldfast fingerprint  | yes  | yes  | yes  | yes  | yes (not if `{}`)  | yes  | yes\nAgentAvow v1  | yes  | yes  | yes  | yes  | yes  | yes  | no\nrugsnare `hash`  | yes  | no  | yes  | yes  | own hash  | compared, not hashed  | yes\nVercel AI SDK `fingerprintTools`  | map key  | yes  | yes  | yes¹  | no  | title only  | no\n\n¹ `@ai-sdk/mcp` sets `additionalProperties: false` on every input schema before the fingerprint is taken, so a change to that member does not move it.\n\nWe implemented the heldfast, AgentAvow and Vercel rules from their published descriptions and checked them against the projects' own test material: heldfast's 14 golden tools, AgentAvow's 3 signed digests and 13 key encodings, and `fingerprintTools` from `ai@7.0.159` on 6,882 tools. All agree. Any of these digests can be computed from a record that keeps the full definition.\n\n## Same servers, same day\n\n23,190 servers appear in both records with a tool list. Another 3,324 were read by both, but on 26 October HISTOR had no digest for their tool sets: most declare fractional numbers in their schemas, which its digest rule refused until 31 October. They are outside this comparison. For 21,215 of the 23,190 HISTOR holds every field heldfast hashes (it keeps title and annotations since 22 October). On those:\n\n  * 20,771 servers (97.9%) are identical tool by tool;\n  * of 318,389 tools, 315,605 (99.1%) have the same heldfast fingerprint.\n\nTwo crawlers built separately agree on 99.1% of the definitions byte for byte. The remaining servers are where the interesting part is.\n\n## Why the other 444 differ\n\nservers  | first reason that applies\n--- | ---\n218  | different npm versions were run\n65  | same definitions, read at different moments\n50  | heldfast's later reads failed, so its copy is older\n33  | the hosted server was edited between the two reads\n24  | the definition contains the reader's own environment\n24  | same npm version, run with network by one reader and without by the other\n15  | the server sent no outputSchema/title to one reader's request\n10  | only the first page of `tools/list` was read\n5  | same npm version, offline on both sides\n\nEvery one of the 33 edited servers was checked live on 31 October. Where today's definitions match one of the two copies (15 servers), they match the copy of whichever reader came later.\n\n78 servers differ only in how they were read, not because the servers changed.\n\nThe definition contains the reader. Some packages put their environment into tool text. `@sunkimkk/pinterest-mcp@1.2.0` describes its download folder as `/tmp/churn-…/Downloads/…` to heldfast and `/home/histor/Downloads/…` to us. `airweave-mcp-search@0.9.62` names its tool after an environment value, so the tool is `search-heldfast-churn-placeholder` for one reader and `search-histor-envkey-…` for the other. Others default a date parameter to today or a field to a fresh UUID. Every reader gets its own digest for these servers, and every user does too.\n\nThe network. Some packages build part of their tool list from a remote service. heldfast ran npm packages with network until 29 September and without it since; HISTOR runs them without. For 24 packages at the same version, the September copy at heldfast differs from ours: richer annotations, other prices in the descriptions, more tools.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDonna Karan New York Petite Zip-Back Pencil Skirt - Macy's (https://www.macys.com/shop/product/donna-karan-new-york-petite-zip-back-pencil-skirt?ID=23512759)\nciteturn5search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Web ID: 23512759\n\n  * Image: Donna Karan New York - Petite Zip-Back Pencil Skirt\n\n# Donna Karan New YorkPetite Zip-Back Pencil Skirt\n\nSorry, this item is currently unavailable.\n\nShop Similar Items\n\nShop All Donna Karan New York\n\n## Product details\n\nWeb ID: 23512759\n\nShape a refined look instantly with this petite textured pencil skirt from Donna Karan New York, a versatile piece that adds sophisticated detail to any work look.\n\n  * ### Product Features\n\n    * Approx. length: 24\"; Pencil silhouette\n    * Designed to fit and flatter frames 5'4\" and under\n    * Back zip closure\n    * Lined\n    * Polyester/acrylic/elastane/metallic; lining: polyester/elastane\n    * Dry clean\n    * Imported\n\n  * ### Shipping & Returns\n\n    * This item qualifies for Free Shipping with minimum purchase! exclusions & details\n    * Our Normal Gift Boxing is not available for this item.\n    * Enjoy a longer window to return most of your holiday purchases. See our Extended Holiday Return Policy to see if this item qualifies.\n    * California and Minnesota customers call 1-800-289-6229 for Free Shipping information.\n    * Returns are accepted at any Macy's store within 30 days from purchase date. Last Act items are final sale and sold \"as is.\" No returns, exchanges, or price adjustments allowed.\n    * For